;
AUTH - CALLER PERMISSIONS

A - Reading the permission map
B - Changing permissions
C - Approving executable programs
:A
READING THE PERMISSION MAP

Enter AUTH from private CP/M with BYE attached.
AUTH /MAP displays the entire map; AUTH J: displays one drive.

An asterisk indicates an explicit DU setting. Otherwise the DU
inherits the drive setting. An explicit DU denial takes precedence.

Shipped defaults:
A0: and I14:    Read only
I0:             Read/write
J: and P:       Read only
Other DUs       No access

Keep SYSUTLS: and the private RBBS areas protected.
:B
CHANGING PERMISSIONS

AUTH J: /R       Read only
AUTH J: /RW      Read and write
AUTH J: /W       Write only
AUTH J: /NONE    Neither
AUTH J3: NONE    Explicit denial of J3:
AUTH J3: INHERIT Remove the DU override

Each argument replaces the entire permission pair.
Changing a drive does not erase its DU overrides. Be particularly
careful with I:, whose private areas inherit the drive's setting
unless explicitly denied.

Changes take effect immediately. AUTH /SAVE saves the running map.
See HELP POLICY for details.
:C
APPROVING EXECUTABLE PROGRAMS

An ordinary COM file must be in a caller-readable, non-writable DU
and have F4 set before a caller can run it.

AUTH J2:EXAMPLE.COM      Examine executable status
AUTH J2:EXAMPLE.COM +X   Approve
AUTH J2:EXAMPLE.COM -X   Withdraw approval

Use an exact DU and COM filename. F4 changes are written directly
to disk and do not need AUTH /SAVE. F4 is not a substitute for
the required DU permissions.

Review the actual program, including its loaders and shell escapes.
BYE is not a sandbox for arbitrary machine code. Clear inherited
F4 flags on imported programs before deliberately approving them.
